 "summary": "# Security Analysis Report: `filelock-3.20.0`\n\n## Package Overview\n`filelock` (v3.20.0) is a widely used, platform-independent Python library providing file-based locking primitives (`UnixFileLock`, `WindowsFileLock`, `SoftFileLock`, and async equivalents) for inter-process synchronization across shared directories, caches, and system resources. The codebase consists of 9 Python source files (~847 lines of code) with zero external runtime dependencies outside the Python standard library.\n\n---\n\n## Key Security Findings\n\n### 1. Symlink Following and TOCTOU Race Condition Leading to Arbitrary File Truncation & Permission Modification\n- **Severity**: High\n- **CWEs**: CWE-59 (Improper Link Resolution Before File Access), CWE-367 (Time-of-Check Time-of-Use Race Condition)\n- **Locations**:\n  - `src/filelock/_unix.py` (Lines 42–46 in `UnixFileLock._acquire`)\n  - `src/filelock/_windows.py` (Line 28 in `WindowsFileLock._acquire`)\n  - `src/filelock/_soft.py` (Line 27 in `SoftFileLock._acquire`)\n  - `src/filelock/_util.py` (Lines 22–36 in `raise_on_not_writable_file`)\n\n#### Vulnerability Mechanics\n1. **Unix Lock Acquisition (`src/filelock/_unix.py`)**:\n   - In `UnixFileLock._acquire`, the code checks whether the lock file already exists via `Path(lock_filename).exists()` (Line 42) before constructing `open_flags` (`os.O_RDWR | os.O_TRUNC`, conditionally adding `os.O_CREAT` if the file did not exist).\n   - Immediately afterward (Line 44), `os.open(lock_filename, open_flags, self._context.mode)` is invoked **without `os.O_NOFOLLOW`**.\n   - Once opened, `os.fchmod(fd, self._context.mode)` is called (Line 46) on the resulting file descriptor.\n   - **TOCTOU & Symlink Impact**: There is a Time-of-Check Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) window between `Path(lock_filename).exists()` and `os.open(...)`. Even without winning a race, if an attacker pre-creates a symbolic link at `lock_filename` in a shared or world-writable directory (such as `/tmp` or a shared cache directory):\n     - If the symlink points to an existing file, `Path.exists()` returns `True`, and `os.open` opens the target file with `O_RDWR | O_TRUNC`, **truncating the target file's contents**.\n     - If the symlink is dangling (points to a non-existent file), `Path.exists()` returns `False`, causing `O_CREAT` to be added without `O_EXCL` or `O_NOFOLLOW`, allowing **arbitrary file creation/overwrite** at the symlink destination.\n     - Subsequent execution of `os.fchmod(fd, self._context.mode)` modifies the permissions of the symlink target file if owned by the executing user.\n\n2. **Windows & Soft Lock Acquisition (`src/filelock/_windows.py`, `src/filelock/_soft.py`, `src/filelock/_util.py`)**:\n   - `WindowsFileLock._acquire` calls `raise_on_not_writable_file(lock_filename)` (which performs `os.stat`, following symlinks/junctions) followed by `os.open(lock_filename, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, self._context.mode)` (Line 28). This introduces a TOCTOU window and follows NTFS symlinks/reparse points, truncating target files via `O_TRUNC`.\n   - `SoftFileLock._acquire` uses `os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_TRUNC` without `os.O_NOFOLLOW` after calling `raise_on_not_writable_file` (which uses `os.stat` rather than `os.lstat`). While `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` prevents following symlinks on modern POSIX kernels, explicit `O_NOFOLLOW` and `lstat` checks are missing.\n\n#### Remediation Recommendations\n- **Include `os.O_NOFOLLOW`**: On platforms supporting `os.O_NOFOLLOW` (POSIX systems), unconditionally include `getattr(os, \"O_NOFOLLOW\", 0)` in `open_flags` when calling `os.open()`.\n- **Eliminate TOCTOU `exists()` Check & Avoid `O_TRUNC`**: Remove the pre-open `Path(lock_filename).exists()` check in `_unix.py` and `raise_on_not_writable_file` pre-checks. Lock files do not need `os.O_TRUNC` for `flock`/`msvcrt.locking` coordination; removing `O_TRUNC` prevents destructive data loss if an existing file is targeted.\n- **Validate File Descriptor Metadata**: After opening `fd`, use `os.fstat(fd)` and `stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode)` to verify that the opened descriptor refers to a regular file, and restrict `os.fchmod` so it only applies when the lock file was newly created or verified safe.\n\n---\n\n## Other Vulnerability Classes Evaluated\n- **Command / Code Injection (CWE-78, CWE-94)**: Clean. No usage of `subprocess`, `os.system`, `eval`, or `exec`.\n- **Unsafe Deserialization (CWE-502)**: Clean. No serialization libraries (`pickle`, `yaml`, `marshal`) are imported or used.\n- **Network / SSRF / Cryptography (CWE-918, CWE-327)**: Not applicable; purely local filesystem synchronization library.",
